 ],
 "literal": "Young people and old people to other lands to go, / when they confronted, you do not rise, chest on the earth to embrace, / staying in the middle of the desert path, O warrior! Pale / without bangles empty hand raising, among relations, / 'Like this he became, the young man,' saying, / if your news reaches, then, / in the ancient village, the mature fruit-bearing banyan tree / full of birds, prosperous, is like that indeed, 'My son / is our wealth and greatness,' saying, every day / without ceasing praising mother, / how will she become, indeed, the pitiable woman?",
 "translation": "While young and old alike flee to other lands, you do not rise to confront them, but lie embracing the earth with your chest, O warrior who has fallen in the middle of the desert path! If the news reaches your kin—of how your young wife, raising her pale, empty hands stripped of bangles, cried out, 'The young man has become like this!'—then how will his mother survive? She who, like the bird-filled, prosperous banyan tree of an ancient village, used to praise him every single day without ceasing, saying, 'My son is our wealth and our pride!' How pitiable she is!",
 "modern": "இளைஞர்களும் முதியவர்களும் வேறு இடங்களுக்குத் தப்பி ஓட, பகைவர்கள் எதிர்க்கும்போது நீ எழாமல், மார்பு மண்ணைத் தழுவப் பாலைவன வழியில் விழுந்து கிடக்கும் வீரனே! வளையல்கள் இல்லாத வெற்றுக்கையை உயர்த்தி, உறவினர்களிடையே 'இளையோன் இப்படி ஆகிவிட்டானே' என்று உன் மனைவி கதறும் செய்தி உன் ஊரைச் சென்றடைந்தால், 'என் மகனே எங்களுக்குச் செல்வமும் பெருமையும்' என்று நாள்தோறும் இடைவிடாது புகழ்ந்து பேசிய அவனது தாய் என்ன ஆவாளோ? பாவம், அவள் மிகவும் இரங்கத்தக்கவள்!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Puram theme of Mutupālai (the desolate desert path of death). A passerby or a fellow warrior addresses a fallen hero who lies dead on the desert path after a battle. The speaker laments that when the news of his death reaches his home, his young wife will raise her bare, bangle-less arms in grief. Most of all, the speaker worries about the hero's mother, who used to boast daily about her son being the family's sole wealth and pride. The mother's pride is compared to a grand, ancient banyan tree that provides shelter and fruit to countless birds. The key words are 'மள்ள' (O warrior), 'வறுங்கை' (empty hand), and 'அன்னை' (mother).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
